Abstract

Continuous monitoring of vital signs is essential for early detection of health abnormalities, especially for patients in remote areas, elderly individuals, and those requiring long-term observation. This project presents the design and implementation of a Smart Healthcare Monitoring System based on the ESP32-C3 microcontroller, which measures key physiological and environmental parameters in real time using low-cost sensors.

The system integrates a DHT22 sensor to measure ambient temperature and humidity, a heart rate (BPM) sensor to monitor pulse rate, and a SpO₂ sensor to measure blood oxygen saturation. The ESP32-C3, a low-power 32-bit RISC-V microcontroller with built-in Wi-Fi and Bluetooth Low Energy, acquires and processes the sensor data, filters the readings, and compares them against predefined threshold values for normal and abnormal conditions. The processed data is displayed locally and transmitted wirelessly over Wi-Fi to a cloud or web-based dashboard, allowing doctors, caregivers, and family members to monitor the patient's condition remotely.

When any parameter goes beyond its safe range, such as low oxygen saturation, abnormal heart rate, or unfavorable room conditions, the system generates an alert to enable timely medical intervention. The integrated wireless connectivity of the ESP32-C3 removes the need for external communication modules, keeping the design compact, cost-effective, and energy-efficient. This makes the system suitable for home healthcare, rural clinics, and telemedicine applications. Experimental results show that the system provides reliable and consistent measurements, demonstrating its potential as a practical IoT-based solution for improving patient care and reducing the need for frequent hospital visits.
